'''Vetulocystidae''' is the only [[family (biology)|family]] of the [[taxon]] '''Vetulocystida''', which is a group of extinct [[deuterostome]]s of uncertain [[phylogeny|phylogenetic position]]. Vetulocystidae is made up of the [[genera]] ''[[Vetulocystis]]'', ''[[Dianchicystis]]'' and ''[[Thylacocercus]]''.<ref name=Shu2004>{{harvnb|Shu|Conway Morris|Han|Zhang|2004|pages=422–423}}</ref><ref name=CM2015>{{harvnb|Conway Morris|Halgedahl|Selden|Jarrard|2015|pages=4–5}}</ref>

==Etymology==
The latin ''vetus'' means "old," while ''cystis'' refers to the organisms' bag-like shape.<ref name=Shu2004 />

==Description==
The body consists of a voluminous anterior [[theca#zoology|theca]], and a smaller posterior section attached to the substrate.<ref name=Shu2004 />  On ''Vetulocystis'' and ''Dianchicystis'', the anterior section features three structures: an antero-dorsal presumed to be the mouth, a left postero-dorsal cone assumed to be the anus (possibly with additional functions), and a right postero-dorsal lenticular respiratory organ.  These structures are not apparent on ''Thylacocercus'', which could either be due to how the sole fossil of that genus was preserved, or due to the mouth opening having migrated inside the crown of tentacle-like structures, as suggested by markings interpreted as an esophagus, and the anal opening having lost 

The anterior cone-shaped structure is believed to have constituted the mouth, due to its similarity to primitive [[echinoderms]] (such as [[stylophora]]ns and [[blastoid]]s); while the posterior structure (similar to that of the [[cystoid]]s and [[eocrinoid]]s) fulfilled the functions of anus and gonopore.<ref>{{harvnb|Shu|Conway Morris|Han|Zhang|2004|pages=425–426}}</ref> Furthermore, and unlike many other echinoderms, these animals lacked a calcified skeleton.<ref name=NotEchin>{{harvnb|Rahman|Zamora|2024|page=301}}</ref>

==Paleoecology==
They were probably [[Sessility (motility)|sessile]] and fed by filtration, although it is possible that the part attached to the substrate produced a slow displacement, at least for ''Vetulocystis'' and ''Dianchicystis''.<ref name=Shu2004 />  ''Thylacocercus'' shows evidence of a holdfast at the end of its posterior section.<ref name=CM2015 />

''[[Vetulocystis catenata]]'' specimens have been found in close proximity to specimens of the small [[vetulicolid]] ''[[Beidazoon venustum]]''.<ref>{{harvnb|Han|Shu|Zhang|Liu|2006|page=2487–2488}}</ref>

==Phylogeny==
The affinities of vetulocystida remain unclear,<ref name=NotEchin /> and the only cladistic analysis of the taxon was done as an extension of work focused on stem-chordates.<ref name=Mussini2024>{{harvnb|Mussini|Smith|Vinther|Rahman|Murdock|Harper|Dunn|2024|loc=supplemental data figure S4}}</ref>

===Internal phylogeny===
''Thylacocercus'' may be more derived than ''Vetulocystis'' and ''Dianchicystis'', which its higher stratigraphic position would support.<ref name=CM2015 />  The only available phylogenetic analysis of vetulocystids noted that the interpretation of their morphology "remains highly contested," but found weak support for the following arrangement:<ref>{{harvnb|Mussini|Smith|Vinther|Rahman|Murdock|Harper|Dunn|2024|loc=supplemental document "Phylogenetic analyses and character descriptions. Related to the STAR methods and Figures 4 and S2-S4.", page 2}}</ref>

